# TASK-260715-ypo7yo — handoff evidence, round 4 (verdict-03 rework)

Revision **2026-07-28.r4**. Status: **ready for review**. No portal mutation has
occurred and none is authorized by this handoff.

Two independent inputs drove this round: reviewer verdict 03 (one blocking
finding, one secondary correction), and the `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` **r2 amendment
packet** (M1–M7), which landed on this task's progress notes after verdict 03 was
written. That task is now `done`, which is what makes the finding answerable
rather than deferrable.

## 1. Verdict 03 F1 — and why neither of the reviewer's two exits was taken verbatim

**The finding is CONFIRMED.** r3 granted
`com.apple.security.application-groups` on both macOS production targets while
`appGroupPurposeRule.macOS` said *"the channel … **will** use"* and the rationale
ended *"so C1 need not be repeated"* — a possible future requirement plus an
operational convenience, exactly as the reviewer characterised it.

The reviewer offered two exits: prove the selected transport needs it, or withhold
pending `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` and re-order C1. **Neither applies as written**,
because that task finished in the interim and its amendment **M6** handed this
exact call here:

> "The entitlement is left granted because a private root-context container may
> still have uses this task did not survey; **whether it survives least privilege
> is a separate review under `ypo7yo`'s ownership**, not a verdict this task is
> competent to issue."

So the survey was done rather than the question deferred a second time. The
outcome is the reviewer's *second* exit taken to its conclusion: **withheld, and
settled** — not withheld-and-pending.

### The survey (matrix §4.3 D-7, JSON `appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule`)

What the entitlement grants on macOS is not a matter of interpretation: it is two
`sandbox-array-entitlement` blocks in `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb`,
the only two places the key appears. Both were read in full on macOS 26.5 (25F71).

| # | grant | lines | namespace | crosses root↔user? | used? |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | group container read/write + extensions | 305–315 | home-relative | no | no |
| 2 | `network-bind` / `network-outbound` in the group container | 307–314 | home-relative | no | no |
| 3 | `/Library/Application Support/AppStore/GroupContent/<suite>` | 316–321 | **system-wide** | yes | no — Mac App Store only |
| 4 | `mach-lookup` / `mach-register` on `global-name-prefix "<suite>."` | 326–328 | **system-wide** | yes | no — XPC candidate **rejected** |
| 5 | `ipc-posix*` on `ipc-posix-name-prefix "<suite>/"` | 329 | **system-wide** | yes | no — never proposed |
| 6 | `~/Library/Application Scripts/<suite>` | 459–465 | home-relative | no | no |

Selected transport (candidate D): system-domain keychain item, seeded over
`sendProviderMessage`, non-secret snapshot in `providerConfiguration`. **None of
the six is an App Group mechanism.**

**M6's open question is answered, and more strongly than M6 expected.**
`(appsandbox-container-common)` / `(appsandbox-container-macos)` at
`application.sb:107-108` sit at top level, inside no `(when (entitlement …))`
form — every sandboxed target already gets its own private container with no App
Group entitlement. A private group container is therefore **redundant**, not
merely unused: the only thing it adds is *sharing*, which is what the root/user
split breaks.

**Verdict: `prohibited` on all four macOS targets, `required` on all four iOS
targets** (rule **G4**), with three narrow, grant-specific reopening conditions.

### The ceremony argument evaporates rather than being demoted

The reviewer's required rework item 2 said ceremony planning must follow the
least-privilege decision. It does, and there is nothing left to weigh: C1's
`explicitlyNotAuthorized` already lists all four iOS App IDs under ADR-024, so iOS
provisioning **already** requires a later portal sitting — and the two App Group
records' only consumers are those same four iOS App IDs. **C1 now creates no App
Group record and enables the App Groups capability on no App ID.** The ceremony
shrank; no sitting was added.

## 2. Beyond the literal ask — two things, both stated

1. **The macOS probe rows were withheld too.** Verdict 03's gate covered only
   `macos.host` and `macos.provider`. Leaving `macos.probe.host` /
   `macos.probe.provider` granted would have parked the identical defect one row
   over, and `TASK-260715-1r0fxv`'s scope and AC name no App Group function.
   Negative gate *"the same defect one row over"* enforces it. This is the
   reviewer's judgement to reverse if the probe should instead prove that an
   iOS-style record can be authorised in a Mac Development profile — that is the
   only argument for the probe grant, and it is a **verification** function, not a
   convenience. It was not adopted because no board element asks for it.
2. **The `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` amendment packet was applied in the same revision**
   (M1–M7), because M1 and F1 touch the same two target rows and splitting them
   would have produced a contract that was internally inconsistent between
   revisions. Detail in §3.

No board element was created, renamed, deleted, or re-linked.

## 3. The amendment packet, item by item

| # | requested | done | where |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| M1 | `macos.provider` `keychain-access-groups`: status → `prohibited`; settled rationale; evidence extended; **`resolutionOwner` and `amendmentRule` deleted** | yes, all five fields | JSON row; §4.2 **D-2** |
| M2 | close **OC-1**, naming the resolver | yes | JSON `openConstraints`; §8 |
| M3 | downgrade **OC-4** to verify-only-if-XPC-is-ever-selected | yes, **plus new evidence** | §8 |
| M4 | `macos.host` keychain — recommend keep granted, purpose narrowed | accepted as recommended | §4.4 |
| M5 | record the replacement rule for the deleted `amendmentRule` | yes — **rule K1** | §4.4 |
| M6 | `application-groups` purpose is false on macOS; §10 traceability row is false | yes — and the grant itself did not survive least privilege | §4.3 D-7, §10 |
| M7 | bump `revision`, append `revisionLog`, keep the validator at 0, **check the deletions do not trip an exhaustive assertion**; consider a gate for a `prohibited` row still carrying a `resolutionOwner` | yes on all; the containment assertions did **not** encode the open state, so nothing had to be relaxed. The suggested gate exists as **R13** in both directions | §9.2 |

M7's warning was worth taking seriously and turned out not to bite: A10a–A10e key
off entitlement *status*, not off row metadata, so deleting `resolutionOwner` and
`amendmentRule` changed no allowlist. R13 now rejects a settled row that keeps
either field — the defect class M1 was fixing — and R29 requires a settled row to
state a reopening condition instead.

## 4. Secondary correction

r3's §9.1 said the `macos.host` development allowlist resolved to "exactly seven
keys" over a list of ten. Fixed — **and the number moved underneath it**: the App
Group is no longer authored on that target, so the list is now **six authored + three
injected = nine** on `development`, **eight** on `developer-id`. Both the arithmetic
error and the new list are stated in §9.1.

## 5. Gates

| gate | result |
| --- | --- |
| reviewer's own verdict-03 `jq` targeted gate | **`true`, exit 0** (verdict 03 recorded `false`, exit 1) |
| `validate_matrix.py` (R1–R30) | **801 checks, PASS, exit 0** (r3: 598 over R1–R27) |
| `mutate.py` structural negative gates | **66/66 hold, exit 0** (r3: 41/41) |
| r3 decision preservation | **15/15 explicit `jq` assertions HOLD** |
| `scripts/check-legacy-preservation.sh` | **PASS, exit 0** |
| `task-board validate` | **Board is valid. No issues found.** exit 0 |
| `swift test` | **335 tests in 29 suites passed, exit 0** — unchanged; no product source was touched |

Both harness scripts were re-run **in place** from
`.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/` and pass there (801 checks / 66 gates,
both exit 0), so the reviewer can reproduce without renaming anything. Full
transcript with exit codes: `TASK-260715-ypo7yo_validate-matrix-04.log`.

**The 25 new negative gates.** Fifteen attack F1: the macOS host re-granted, the
macOS provider re-granted, the *probe* host re-granted, an **iOS row withheld**
(the over-correction — the gate must fail in both directions), a withheld row
keeping its group literal, a withheld row dropping its reopening condition, a
withheld row still claiming the portal capability, a granted row dropping its
stated function, rule G2 reverted to r3's future-conditional wording, the survey
conceding a grant *is* used, the survey trimmed to hide the system-wide grants, C1
re-enabling the capability, C1 re-authorizing a record, a record claiming C1
authorization, a deferred record dropping its allocator. Nine attack the amendment
packet (the settled keychain row keeping `resolutionOwner`, keeping the deleted
`amendmentRule`, sliding back to pending, being re-granted, dropping its reopening
condition; K1 losing its TN3137 basis; OC-1 reopened, closed without a resolver,
closed while still naming a pending owner). One deletes assertion A16.

**One gate caught this author.** R28 rejects conditional wording in a *granted*
row's rationale. The first draft of the iOS probe rationale contained the phrase
"the same shared-container path it **will use** in production" and the validator
failed it. The wording was fixed rather than the rule loosened — the rule is doing
what verdict 03 asked for.

## 6. Preserved decisions

Verdict 03 named what was acceptable. All fifteen assertions over the r4 contract
hold (log §4): the four production identifiers and reciprocal containment;
channel-keyed NE values; the iOS-style App Group **records** and identical
literals; `macos.provider` keychain withholding; team-prefix handling; the legacy
`works.relux.proxy` no-migration rule; the Sparkle exception on `macos.host` alone
and rule X1's register and banned set; C1's App ID list, its iOS exclusions, its
development-only channel and unsuffixed values; probe profile classes; environment
identity rules; group disjointness; the amendment rule and consumer list.

Board resources are not git-tracked, so no pristine r3 JSON survives to diff
against; preservation is demonstrated by those assertions plus the 41 retained r3
negative gates, all still holding.

## 7. Acceptance criteria

| AC | status | evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 — exact four identifiers and containment | met, unchanged since r2 | §2; R1–R5; P1/P2 |
| 2 — only required NE / App Group / Keychain entitlements per target, every host↔provider difference explained | met, **now genuinely least-privilege on macOS** | §4.2 D-1…D-7; §4.3 G4; §4.4 K1; R28, R29 |
| 3 — team prefix, debug vs release, profile type, cross-platform rules explicit and machine-checkable | met, **strengthened** | §1, §5, §6, §7; A1–A16; R1–R30; 66 negative gates |
| 4 — no collision with the shipped macOS bundle, migration called out | met, unchanged | §3; R4; `check-legacy-preservation.sh` exit 0 |
| 5 — independent architecture reviewer accepts before portal mutation | **pending this review** | verdicts 01–03 addressed; no portal mutation performed |

## 8. Review focus

1. **Is withholding the macOS App Group the right call, or should the *probe*
   pair keep it to prove at Gate P0 that an iOS-style record can be authorised in
   a Mac Development profile?** That is the one genuine counter-argument (§2 item
   1). It was not adopted because no board element asks for it, and rule G1's bet
   is only load-bearing for iOS, which C1 does not touch.
2. **Are the three reopening conditions in D-7 the right set?** They are
   deliberately grant-specific (Mach prefix / POSIX IPC / provider moved to a user
   context) so that "the spec mentions App Groups" cannot reopen the row.
3. **R30 makes C1 scope derived rather than asserted.** Check that deriving the
   capability list from target requirements is the behaviour `TASK-260728-q5kjta`
   wants, since it shrinks that task's step 3.
4. `PlatformVPNIdentity.appGroupIdentifier` is now unsatisfiable on macOS
   (§10.1). It is recorded for `TASK-260715-1tzaed`, not fixed here — confirm that
   is the right boundary.

## 9. Scope discipline

No board element created, renamed, deleted, or re-linked. No product source, spec
file, or generated project modified — the `PlatformVPNIdentity` and
`.spec/architecture.md:111` divergences are **recorded** in §10.1 with named
owners, not actioned. No secret value, key path, key ID, issuer ID, or account
credential appears in any artifact; third-party bundle identifiers and Team IDs
quoted as evidence are public and readable from any installed copy of those apps.
The sandbox profile quoted is a world-readable system file.

This was autonomous architecture-contract rework — not a Stop-The-Line condition,
and no human decision was required.
# TASK-260715-ypo7yo — independent architecture review verdict 03

Verdict: **CHANGES REQUESTED**.
Review route: **analysis**.
Reviewed 2026-07-28 as reviewer. No portal mutation is authorized by this
verdict.

## Blocking finding

### F1 — macOS App Groups are pre-granted before they have a required function

The matrix correctly establishes that a user-context macOS containing app and a
root-context Network Extension system extension do not share the same App Group
container. It then assigns
`com.apple.security.application-groups = ["group.works.relux.tunnel"]` to both
macOS production targets as `required`, even though:

- `appGroupPurposeRule.macOS` says the group is retained for the channel that
  `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` **will** select;
- the gap task explicitly compares transports including
  `NETunnelProviderSession` app-provider messaging and XPC, so an App Group
  namespace is not established as a requirement of every viable transport;
- OC-4 says the App Group style question has matrix effect **"none today"**; and
- the stated rationale includes avoiding a repeated Ceremony C1, which is an
  operational convenience rather than a least-privilege requirement.

Apple's Network Extension guidance distinguishes app-provider messages, which
are supported directly by Network Extension, from App Group based state/IPC.
Apple also confirms that a system extension runs as root and therefore receives
a different App Group container from its user-context containing app. App
Groups can authorize Mach IPC and other communication, but only once the
selected design actually uses such a channel:

- https://developer.apple.com/forums/tags/networkextension
- https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/configuring-app-groups

Independent targeted gate over the authoritative JSON:

```sh
jq -e '
  ((.appGroupPurposeRule.macOS
      | contains("selected by TASK-260728-7ii1xz will use"))
    and
    ((.openConstraints[] | select(.id == "OC-4") | .matrixEffect)
      | startswith("none today"))) as $future_only
  |
  ([.targets[]
    | select(.key == "macos.host" or .key == "macos.provider")
    | .entitlements["com.apple.security.application-groups"].status]
    | all(. != "required"))
  or ($future_only | not)
' TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json
```

Output: `false`. Exit code: **1**.

Impact: AC2 and the least-privilege Definition of Done fail. The current
contract authorizes a portal capability and signed entitlement based on a
possible future transport rather than a concrete target requirement.

## Required rework

1. Resolve the macOS App Group grant before Ceremony C1:
   - either cite the selected, current macOS host/provider transport and prove
     why that transport requires this exact App Group entitlement on each macOS
     target; or
   - mark the macOS rows withheld pending `TASK-260728-7ii1xz`, then update C1
     scope and dependency ordering so portal mutation cannot pre-grant them.
2. Do not use avoiding another human ceremony as the capability rationale.
   Ceremony/dependency planning must follow the least-privilege decision.
3. Add a validator rule and adversarial mutation that fail when a macOS App
   Group is `required` while its only named function remains conditional on an
   unresolved transport decision.
4. Update the JSON, Markdown, handoff evidence, and logbook consistently while
   preserving the accepted identifier, containment, Network Extension,
   Keychain-withholding, legacy, Sparkle-exception, profile, and C1-exclusion
   decisions.

## Secondary correction

Markdown section 9.1 says the `macos.host` development allowlist resolves to
"exactly seven keys", but the list contains seven authored keys plus two
always-generated keys plus one development-only key: **10 total**. The JSON is
internally correct; fix the prose count during rework.

